body { font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif; font-size: 12pt; line-height: 1.4; margin: 20px; }
.header { text-align: center; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 20px; border-bottom: 2px solid #000; padding-bottom: 10px; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class='header'>
    <h2>RRB Group of Schools</h2>
    <h3>Half Yearly — Chemistry (Class 12 A)</h3>
</div>
<table width='100%' style='margin-bottom:15px;font-weight:bold;'>
    <tr>
        <td>Time Allowed: 3 Hours</td>
        <td align='right'>Maximum Marks: 70</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Teacher: GAURAV SHUKLA</td>
        <td align='right'>Date: 21/08/2026</td>
    </tr>
</table>
<hr>
<h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Select the most appropriate option for each of the following questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Which of the following alkyl halides undergoes S<sub>N</sub><sub>1</sub> reaction most rapidly?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) CH<sub>3</sub>-Cl</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH-Cl</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-Cl</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-Cl</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Phenol reacts with concentrated HNO<sub>3</sub> in the presence of concentrated H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> to form which major product?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) o-Nitrophenol</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) p-Nitrophenol</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 2,4,6-Trinitrophenol</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Benzoic acid</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Which of the following compounds will give a positive iodoform test?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Pentan-3-one</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Pentan-2-one</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Benzaldehyde</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Methanol</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> The correct increasing order of basic strength of amines in aqueous medium is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N < CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> < (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> < (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH < (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH < CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> < (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N < (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH < CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> Chlorobenzene on heating with aqueous NaOH solution at 623 K and 300 atm pressure gives phenol. This reaction is known as:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Fittig reaction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Wurtz-Fittig reaction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Dow's process</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Kolbe's reaction</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q6.</strong> Anisole on heating with concentrated HI gives:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Iodobenzene and Methanol</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Phenol and Methyl iodide</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Phenol and Methanol</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Iodobenzene and Methyl iodide</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q7.</strong> Propanone on reduction with zinc amalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid gives propane. This reaction is known as:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Wolff-Kishner reduction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Clemmensen reduction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Stephen reduction</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Rosenmund reduction</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q8.</strong> Which reagent is used to convert an amide into a primary amine containing one carbon atom less than the parent amide?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) LiAlH<sub>4</sub> in ether</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Br<sub>2</sub> + aqueous NaOH</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) PCl<sub>5</sub></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) NaNO<sub>2</sub> + HCl</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q9.</strong> The correct order of increasing acid strength of the given carboxylic acids is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) CH<sub>3</sub>COOH < CH<sub>2</sub>ClCOOH < CHCl<sub>2</sub>COOH < CCl<sub>3</sub>COOH</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) CCl<sub>3</sub>COOH < CHCl<sub>2</sub>COOH < CH<sub>2</sub>ClCOOH < CH<sub>3</sub>COOH</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) CH<sub>3</sub>COOH < CCl<sub>3</sub>COOH < CHCl<sub>2</sub>COOH < CH<sub>2</sub>ClCOOH</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) CH<sub>2</sub>ClCOOH < CH<sub>3</sub>COOH < CHCl<sub>2</sub>COOH < CCl<sub>3</sub>COOH</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q10.</strong> Carbylamine test is given by which class of compounds?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Primary aliphatic and aromatic amines</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Secondary amines only</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Tertiary amines only</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) All aliphatic amines</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q11.</strong> Which of the following compounds will NOT undergo Aldol condensation?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) CH<sub>3</sub>CHO</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) HCHO</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CHO</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q12.</strong> Lucas reagent is a mixture of:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Concentrated HCl and anhydrous ZnCl<sub>2</sub></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Dilute HCl and hydrated ZnCl<sub>2</sub></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Concentrated HNO<sub>3</sub> and anhydrous ZnCl<sub>2</sub></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Concentrated H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> and ZnCl<sub>2</sub></li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section B: Assertion-Reason Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: In the following questions, a statement of Assertion (A) is followed by a statement of Reason (R). Choose the correct option:
(a) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).
(b) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true but Reason (R) is NOT the correct explanation of Assertion (A).
(c) Assertion (A) is true but Reason (R) is false.
(d) Assertion (A) is false but Reason (R) is true.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q13.</strong> Assertion (A): S<sub>N</sub><sub>2</sub> reactions proceed with complete inversion of configuration.
Reason (R): The nucleophile attacks the carbon atom from the side opposite to the leaving group.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q14.</strong> Assertion (A): Phenol is more acidic than ethanol.
Reason (R): Phenoxide ion is resonance stabilized while ethoxide ion is not.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q15.</strong> Assertion (A): Aldehydes and ketones have higher boiling points than hydrocarbons of comparable molecular mass.
Reason (R): Aldehydes and ketones undergo intermolecular hydrogen bonding.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q16.</strong> Assertion (A): Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction.
Reason (R): Aniline forms a salt with anhydrous aluminium chloride, which acts as a Lewis acid.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true.</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section C: Very Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions in brief.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q17.</strong> How will you carry out the following conversions?
(i) Propene to Propan-1-ol
(ii) Propene to Propan-2-ol</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q18.</strong> Give one chemical test to distinguish between the following pairs of compounds:
(i) Phenol and Benzoic acid
(ii) Propanal and Propanone</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q19.</strong> Write the reaction mechanism for Williamson ether synthesis. Why is the reaction of tertiary alkyl halide with sodium ethoxide not suitable for preparing tert-butyl ethyl ether?</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q20.</strong> Write chemical equations for the following named reactions:
(i) Carbylamine reaction
(ii) Hoffmann bromamide degradation reaction</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q21.</strong> Account for the following observations:
(i) Haloarenes are extremely less reactive towards nucleophilic substitution reactions.
(ii) Boiling point of haloalkanes decreases with branching for isomeric alkyl halides.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section D: Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q22.</strong> Differentiate between S<sub>N</sub><sub>1</sub> and S<sub>N</sub><sub>2</sub> mechanisms on the basis of:
(i) Order of reaction and kinetics
(ii) Reactivity order of primary, secondary, and tertiary alkyl halides
(iii) Stereochemical outcome of the reaction</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q23.</strong> Write the major products formed when:
(i) Chlorobenzene is treated with CH<sub>3</sub>Cl in presence of anhydrous AlCl<sub>3</sub>.
(ii) Phenol is treated with chloroform in the presence of aqueous NaOH at 340 K.
(iii) Ethanol is heated with excess concentrated H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> at 443 K.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q24.</strong> An organic compound (A) with molecular formula C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O gives a positive Tollens' test and forms an adduct with NaHSO<sub>3</sub>. Compound (A) on reduction with NaBH<sub>4</sub> gives compound (B). Compound (B) reacts with SOCl<sub>2</sub> to form compound (C).
(i) Identify compounds (A), (B), and (C).
(ii) Write the chemical equations involved.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q25.</strong> Explain the following observations:
(i) pK<sub>b</sub> value of aniline is higher than that of methylamine.
(ii) Primary amines have higher boiling points than tertiary amines of isomeric molecular mass.
(iii) Aliphatic amines are stronger bases than ammonia.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q26.</strong> How will you bring about the following conversions?
(i) Ethanal to But-2-enal
(ii) Benzene to Acetophenone
(iii) Benzoic acid to Benzaldehyde</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q27.</strong> Complete the following chemical reactions:
(i) C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + NaNO<sub>2</sub> + 2HCl  &rarr; [273-278 K] [A]  &rarr; [Cu<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>/HCl] [B]
(ii) CH<sub>3</sub>COOH  &rarr; [P<sub>4</sub>/Br<sub>2</sub>] [C]  &rarr; [alc. NH<sub>3</sub>] [D]
(iii) CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub>  &rarr; [Zn-Hg/conc. HCl] [E]</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q28.</strong> Arrange the following sets of compounds as directed:
(i) CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Cl, (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CHCl, (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>CCl (In increasing order of reactivity towards S<sub>N</sub><sub>2</sub> mechanism)
(ii) Propan-1-ol, Propane-1,2,3-triol, Propan-1,2-diol (In increasing order of boiling points)
(iii) Benzoic acid, 4-Nitrobenzoic acid, 4-Methoxybenzoic acid (In increasing order of acidic strength)</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section E: Long Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following long answer questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q29.</strong> (a) Write the chemical mechanism for the acid-catalyzed dehydration of ethanol to yield ethene at 443 K.
(b) Explain why p-nitrophenol is more acidic than p-methoxyphenol.
(c) Write the chemical reaction for Kolbe's reaction.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q30.</strong> (a) Write short notes with chemical equations on the following:
(i) Cannizzaro reaction
(ii) Cross Aldol condensation
(iii) Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky (HVZ) reaction

(b) Give reasons:
(i) Carboxylic acids do not give characteristic reactions of carbonyl group.
(ii) Benzaldehyde is less reactive than ethanal towards nucleophilic addition reactions.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q31.</strong> (a) An organic compound (A) C<sub>8</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O on oxidation with alkaline KMnO<sub>4</sub> gives monocarboxylic acid (B) which on heating with soda lime gives benzene. Compound (A) on treatment with PCl<sub>5</sub> gives (C), which on reaction with alcoholic KOH forms compound (D). Compound (D) adds HBr in the presence of peroxide to give (E). Identify compounds (A) to (E) and write all chemical equations involved.

(b) How will you distinguish between a primary, secondary, and tertiary amine using Hinsberg's reagent?</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section F: Case Study Based Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Read the following passages carefully and answer the questions that follow.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q32.</strong> Read the text carefully and answer the questions:
Nucleophilic substitution reactions are divided into two main classes based on their kinetic behavior: Bimolecular Nucleophilic Substitution (S<sub>N</sub><sub>2</sub>) and Unimolecular Nucleophilic Substitution (S<sub>N</sub><sub>1</sub>). S<sub>N</sub><sub>2</sub> reactions proceed via a concerted pathway with a single transition state involving both substrate and nucleophile. This results in complete inversion of configuration (Walden inversion). Allylic and benzylic halides show high reactivity towards S<sub>N</sub><sub>1</sub> reactions due to carbocation stabilization through resonance. On the other hand, steric hindrance plays a major role in determining S<sub>N</sub><sub>2</sub> reactivity.</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>Why are primary alkyl halides generally more reactive in S<sub>N</sub><sub>2</sub> reactions compared to tertiary alkyl halides?</li><li>Which compound out of 2-bromobutane and 1-bromobutane is optically active and why?</li><li>Out of C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Cl and C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH(Cl)CH<sub>3</sub>, which one is more reactive towards S<sub>N</sub><sub>1</sub> reaction and why?</li></ol><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q33.</strong> Read the text carefully and answer the questions:
Carboxylic acids are organic compounds containing the carboxyl functional group (-COOH). Due to resonance stabilization of the carboxylate ion, carboxylic acids are significantly more acidic than alcohols and phenols. Electron-withdrawing groups (EWGs) increase the acidity by stabilizing the carboxylate anion through inductive or resonance effects, whereas electron-donating groups (EDGs) decrease the acidity by destabilizing the anion. The acidity of substituted benzoic acids depends both on the nature of the substituent and its position on the benzene ring.</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>Why is formic acid (HCOOH) stronger than acetic acid (CH<sub>3</sub>COOH)?</li><li>Arranged in increasing order of acidity: Fluoroacetic acid, Chloroacetic acid, Bromoacetic acid. Justify.</li><li>Explain why ortho-nitrobenzoic acid is stronger than meta-nitrobenzoic acid and para-nitrobenzoic acid.</li></ol>
</body>
</html>